---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import Breadcrumbs from '../components/Breadcrumbs.astro';
import ScanCta from '../components/ScanCta.astro';
import { breadcrumbs } from '../data/schema';
import { APP_URL } from '../config';
import { MAX_OKAY_ADDED_G as G } from '../data/ingredients';
const crumbs = [{ name: 'Home', href: '/' }, { name: 'FAQ', href: '/faq' }];
// Visible HTML only. Deliberately no FAQPage schema.
---
<Base title="Sugar Depth FAQ" description="Answers about Sugar Depth: how the CLEAN, OKAY and BAD verdicts work, why a 0 g label can still miss CLEAN, why stevia is OKAY, what happens to your photos, and what Sugar Depth doesn't check." path="/faq" jsonLd={[breadcrumbs(crumbs)]}>
  <div class="wrap page-head">
    <Breadcrumbs items={crumbs} />
    <span class="kicker">FAQ</span>
    <h1>Questions, answered plainly.</h1>
  </div>
  <div class="wrap page-body prose">
    <h2 id="what">What is Sugar Depth?</h2>
    <p>A food-label scanner. You photograph the ingredient list on a package and it tells you whether the food has added sugar or sweeteners, as one verdict: CLEAN, OKAY or BAD.</p>

    <h2 id="barcode">Does it scan barcodes?</h2>
    <p>No. Sugar Depth has no product database. It reads the ingredient list printed on the package in front of you, so it works on any label it can read, including store brands and imports.</p>

    <h2 id="zero-grams">Why isn't a label with 0&nbsp;g of sugar CLEAN?</h2>
    <p>Two common reasons. First, artificial sweeteners like <a href="/ingredients/sucralose">sucralose</a> aren't sugar, so they never show up in the sugar lines, but they make a label BAD. Second, US labels may round under 0.5&nbsp;g of sugar down to 0&nbsp;g, so a small amount of listed sugar can still print as zero. A listed added sugar with a printed 0&nbsp;g is OKAY, not CLEAN.</p>

    <h2 id="little">It's only a little sugar. What does it get?</h2>
    <p>OKAY, if the Nutrition Facts panel shows {G}&nbsp;g or less of added sugar per serving. More than {G}&nbsp;g is BAD, and so is added sugar whose grams per serving can't be read. There is no GOOD tier: any added sugar, under any name, keeps a label out of CLEAN. That's a deliberate choice, not a health claim.</p>

    <h2 id="stevia">Why is stevia OKAY instead of CLEAN?</h2>
    <p>Because it's still a sweetener. Plant sweeteners (<a href="/ingredients/stevia">stevia</a>, <a href="/ingredients/monk-fruit">monk fruit</a>, <a href="/ingredients/thaumatin">thaumatin</a>), <a href="/ingredients/allulose">allulose</a> and sugar alcohols like <a href="/ingredients/erythritol">erythritol</a> are OKAY: not added sugar, but added to make the food sweet. Stevia paired with erythritol is still OKAY. Stevia paired with an artificial sweetener is BAD. The scanner's Strict mode is on by default; with it off, a product whose only sweetener is stevia, monk fruit or thaumatin shows as CLEAN.</p>

    <h2 id="fruit">Does fruit or milk count?</h2>
    <p>No. Sugar that comes with whole foods like milk, yogurt, fruit, dried fruit, single-strength juice and fruit puree is allowed in CLEAN. <a href="/ingredients/juice-concentrate">Juice concentrate</a> and lactose listed as its own ingredient are added sugar.</p>

    <h2 id="maltodextrin">Is maltodextrin sugar?</h2>
    <p>No. It's a nonsweet starch polymer under federal rules, and it's allowed in CLEAN. <a href="/ingredients/maltodextrin">Read the maltodextrin guide</a>.</p>

    <h2 id="unclear">What if the photo is blurry or the list is cut off?</h2>
    <p>Sugar Depth asks you to retake the photo rather than guessing. The exception: if the visible part already lists added sugar, a sweetener or alcohol, that decides the verdict (BAD or OKAY), because the hidden part can only add more.</p>

    <h2 id="medical">Is this medical advice?</h2>
    <p>No. The verdicts are Sugar Depth's preferences about sweeteners, not medical or safety findings. FDA permits the sweeteners Sugar Depth flags. Talk to a clinician about your own diet.</p>

    <h2 id="allergens">Does it check allergens?</h2>
    <p>No. Sugar Depth only looks for sugars and sweeteners. Always check the label yourself for allergens.</p>

    <h2 id="photos">What happens to my photo?</h2>
    <p>The photo is sent to Sugar Depth's backend and to AI services that read the label text and return a verdict. The details, including what is kept and for how long, are in the <a href="/privacy">privacy policy</a>.</p>

    <h2 id="signin">How do I sign in?</h2>
    <p>With your Google account, in the scanner at <a href={APP_URL}>{APP_URL.replace('https://', '')}</a>.</p>

    <h2 id="delete">Can I delete my account?</h2>
    <p>Yes. Account deletion is available in the scanner's menu. It deletes your profile and the scan history on your device. See the <a href="/privacy">privacy policy</a>.</p>

    <h2 id="countries">Does it work outside the US?</h2>
    <p>Its ingredient registry is in English. It also knows common words for sugar in several other European languages and EU sweetener E numbers like E955. The label-rule explanations on this site follow US FDA rules.</p>

    <h2 id="wrong">I got a wrong verdict. What now?</h2>
    <p>Please <a href="/contact">email a photo of the ingredient list</a> and the verdict you got. Missing ingredient names get added to the registry.</p>

    <div style="margin-top:48px"><ScanCta /></div>
  </div>
</Base>
